WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
THE STATE OF TIPPING:
Tipping has become a burden. 25%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:12.180
50%, 75% tips for a takeout order or Starbucks&nbsp;
coffee. And don’t even get me started on food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.220
delivery apps. If you’re ordering something small,&nbsp;
be prepared to leave a tip that’s worth more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:23.100
your order itself. It’s not just the restaurants&nbsp;
where you’ll be tipping either. Nowadays, tipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.640
is commonplace at barbershops, car washes, ubers,&nbsp;
plumbers, and basically any other service that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.160
can think of which doesn’t really make sense.
At least with restaurants, the service is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.020
the main thing that you’re paying for. You’re&nbsp;
paying for the food, so it kind of makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.300
to appreciate great service that came along with&nbsp;
it. But, in the case of a car wash or a hair cut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.840
you’re paying for the service itself, so it’s&nbsp;
kind of odd that you have to pay 15 or 25%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.840
for an average job, and inflation has only made&nbsp;
this worse. Now, I’m not trying to suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.660
these people shouldn’t be paid a fair wage, but&nbsp;
rather that it should be reflected in the upfront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.700
price. This would not only make it easier for&nbsp;
customers to guage just how much their spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.640
for a service, but it also makes the income of the&nbsp;
service worker much more stable. In fact, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.800
how it is most places outside of the United&nbsp;
States and Canada. In countries like France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:26.640
Denmark, and much of Europe, a standard service&nbsp;
fee of 15% is applied to all bills. So, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.420
no awkward situation where you have to select 15,&nbsp;
25, 35% or no tip as the cashiers watching you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:40.620
but even this is not ideal. Ideally, the price of&nbsp;
service is just included in the product itself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:46.980
is the case in Japan, China, and South Korea.&nbsp;
In fact, in many of these countries, tipping is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.420
actually considered rude and disrespectful.&nbsp;
The reason being that if you’re tipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.520
you’re basically insinuating that the business&nbsp;
doesn’t pay them enough which is unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:02.700
often the case in America. In fact, the minimum&nbsp;
wage for service workers is only $2.13 per hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.540
in America. But, how did things get so bad? How&nbsp;
did tipping go from being something that we use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.120
appreciate exceptional service to being a burden&nbsp;
for customers and a necessity for employees.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.480
A CHECKERED HISTORY:&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.740
Taking a look back, tipping wasn’t&nbsp;
always a custom in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.680
but it has been this way for quite some time as&nbsp;
American tipping dates back to the mid 1800s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.340
You may have heard that tip stands for “To Insure&nbsp;
Promptness”, but that’s just a myth. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.380
tipping was something that wealthy Americans&nbsp;
discovered when traveling to Europe. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.820
tipping was a master-serf custom that was&nbsp;
invented in Europe during the middle ages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:50.460
Masters would reward servants with a bit of extra&nbsp;
cash for excellent service. As these Americans saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:56.460
this practice, they latched onto it and brought&nbsp;
it back to the US. Being able to pay extra for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.760
service really showcased their status and wealth&nbsp;
which many of their egos could not resist. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.400
tipping didn’t catch on in America that easily;&nbsp;
in fact, there was a lot of resistance to tipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.260
For one, people felt that tipping culture&nbsp;
was simply reinforcing social constructs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.420
classicim. The upper class was literally flexing&nbsp;
their wealth on lower class citizens. But, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.180
wasn’t the only concern. The other concern was&nbsp;
that tipping was only viable for the rich. Eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.700
out was expensive as it is, so expecting middle&nbsp;
class and lower class people to offer tips on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:36.180
of the bill seemed “un-American”. Fun fact, this&nbsp;
ideology became so prevalent in America that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:41.640
actually spread to Europe and that’s why tipping&nbsp;
is not standard in European countries. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.820
America would completely u-turn on this idea after&nbsp;
the civil war. While slaves were finally freed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:53.040
their job prospects weren’t all that great and&nbsp;
most of them would end up in service related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.700
jobs. And these industries figured that this was&nbsp;
the perfect opportunity to capitalize on tipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.260
You see, these industries figured that they could&nbsp;
get away with paying former slaves no money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:10.020
Technically, they weren’t enslaving these people&nbsp;
as they could earn money through tips. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:15.420
they could theoretically leave at anytime, but&nbsp;
really it’s not like they had anywhere else to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.460
go. So, service industries became filled with&nbsp;
former slaves that became dependent on tips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:26.400
for income. Suddenly, tipping was no longer a&nbsp;
way to flex your wealth, it became much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.640
of a moral issue. If you didn’t tip, you were&nbsp;
inadvertently supporting slavery. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.840
you could argue that if you buy chocolate or&nbsp;
something, you’re supporting child labor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.640
some 3rd world country. But, it’s a completely&nbsp;
different scenario when you’re face to face with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.380
the person. Essentially, the people who were&nbsp;
against slavery started feeling obliged to tip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:52.380
regardless of their own financial position, and&nbsp;
with that, tipping started becoming a burden.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.560
RESISTANCE FALLS OUT:
While tipping was rapidly gaining popularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.500
through the late 1800s and early 1900s, this isn’t&nbsp;
to say that everyone was happy with the practive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:10.620
In fact, a large portion of the population was&nbsp;
not happy with the practive and we would see some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.600
resistance even from local governments. Georgia,&nbsp;
for example, deemed that tips were essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.900
commercial bribes used to influence service. So,&nbsp;
naturally, paying bribes should not be customary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:28.200
or encouraged. But, the issue was the much of the&nbsp;
push back from these governments were classic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.980
their own ways. For example, in 1915, six states&nbsp;
would abolish the practice of tipping. This seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.060
like a reasonable move. The policy that should&nbsp;
have been introduced to enforce this rule would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.740
have been some sort of minimum wage. This way,&nbsp;
service workers would no longer be dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.860
on tips. But, instead of punishing businesses for&nbsp;
paying $0 incomes, these governments would decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.440
to punish service workers. Iowa, for example,&nbsp;
ruled that those who accepted a gratuity of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.800
kind — not those who gave the money themselves&nbsp;
— could be fined or imprisoned. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.980
supreme courts would step in and deem these rules&nbsp;
unconstitutional, but they never proposed a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:14.100
solution, meaning that things just returned to how&nbsp;
they were. In fact, they actually became worse due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.860
to a series of laws passed by Congress starting&nbsp;
with the Fair Labor Standards Act in 1938. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:25.980
legislation introduced a federal minimum wage, a&nbsp;
40 hour work week, and banned child labor which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:33.600
sound great. But, the pitfall of this act was that&nbsp;
it specifically excluded tipped workers. Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.480
would simply double down on this stance in 1966&nbsp;
with their so called “tip credit”. Essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.640
restaurants were legally allowed to pay a sub&nbsp;
minimum wage as long as tips pushed workers over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.980
the minimum wage threshold. At least, restaurants&nbsp;
were required to pay a percentage of the minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:56.820
wage, usually somewhere between 40 and 60%.&nbsp;
But, this too would change with time. In 1996,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.960
congress ruled that restaurants only&nbsp;
had to pay tipped workers a minimum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.800
$2.13 per hour and that’s still how it is across&nbsp;
much of the US. You would think that with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.500
these anti service worker policies that people&nbsp;
would rise up and protest these changes, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:19.380
exact opposite actually happened. You see, the&nbsp;
demographics of service industries were rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:25.260
changing. It was no longer a bunch of former&nbsp;
slaves. In fact, over 50% of all waiters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.145
now white people. So, while tipping may still be&nbsp;
classicist, it was no longer particularly racist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.840
which eliminated much of the general public’s&nbsp;
hesitancy towards tipping. But, it wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.600
the public that was ok with tipping. Many service&nbsp;
workers actually preferred the tipping model. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:39.120
see, it wasn’t just the race of service workers&nbsp;
that was changing, but also the age. Nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.040
service industries are filled with a bunch of high&nbsp;
school students and young adults. In fact, nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:50.580
50% of all waiters are between the age of 20 and&nbsp;
30. For these people, doing service work is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:56.160
a part time thing so stability isn’t necessarily&nbsp;
the top concern meaning that they’re much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.540
willing to take a gamble. Instead of taking&nbsp;
a set minimum wage, they’re willing to bet on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:05.580
the generosity of people during a Friday night or&nbsp;
Independence Day weekend. Statistically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.680
you’re much better off with a minimum wage, but&nbsp;
this hasn’t exactly changed the minds of service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.500
workers. Tipping was no longer about appreciation&nbsp;
or fighting slavery. Tipping was now about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:22.380
maximizing revenue for service industries and&nbsp;
service workers leading us to where we are today.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.960
THE TIPPING BURDEN:
It turns out that there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.400
whole science behind the psychology of tipping&nbsp;
and there’s dozens of research articles about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.960
how to maximize tips. These studies found&nbsp;
that waiters should introduce themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.940
repeat customer orders, give compliments,&nbsp;
call customers by their name, etc. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:47.940
those are all small fish items. The factor that&nbsp;
has really propagated modern tipping culture is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:53.820
none other than technology. The reality is that&nbsp;
technology makes it so easy to spend money and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.920
has removed so much of the pain that is naturally&nbsp;
associated with spending money that we’re simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:05.040
way more willing to spend money. Take cards for&nbsp;
example. Consumers are willing to spend up to 83%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.280
more when paying with a card as opposed to cash.&nbsp;
So, I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:18.540
the average cash purchase is only $22 while the&nbsp;
average card purchase is $112. But, by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:24.780
biggest culprit when it comes to excessive tipping&nbsp;
is the infamous iPad payment terminals. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.160
you’re paying with a card as opposed to&nbsp;
cash, so that’s already working against you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:34.260
but there’s several other factors that push you&nbsp;
to tip. For example, you don’t have to go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:39.840
the hassle of calculating a tip. You can simply&nbsp;
select a percentage and the iPad will do the math.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.660
Another technique that these terminals employ is&nbsp;
suggesting a super high minimum tip. This terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:53.220
for example is suggesting a minimum tip of 18%.&nbsp;
Of course, you could skip the tip, but what if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:59.160
felt that the service actually deserved a 5 or 10%&nbsp;
tip. You can either type in the tip manually which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:05.400
would just prolong the awkwardness or you can&nbsp;
just select 18. Speaking of awkwardness, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:10.620
likely the most effective method when it comes to&nbsp;
guilting customers into tipping and the source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:16.560
most people’s frustrations. Traditionally, any and&nbsp;
all tips are left on the table and waiters don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:22.440
see the amount until after the customer leaves.&nbsp;
This allows customers be honest about how good the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.080
service actually was. But, when you’re forced to&nbsp;
select a tip amount at the register, you know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.660
the cashier is going to see what you select, but&nbsp;
that’s not the worst part. The worst part is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:39.240
everyone behind you is also going to see what&nbsp;
you select and you don’t want to be the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.180
that skips the tip. And all of this is obviously&nbsp;
by design. Service industries have figued out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:48.720
how to brilliantly leverage technology and peer&nbsp;
pressure to maximize tips. While this is a smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.240
move for them, this has simply left customers&nbsp;
exhausted and frustrated with modern tipping.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.760
THE ANTI TIPPING MOVEMENT:
Hearing all this, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:04.740
that you’d be surprised to hear that there’s&nbsp;
a strong and growing movement against tipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:09.480
These people argue that tipping has nothing to&nbsp;
do with showing appreciation and everything to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:15.240
do with the fact that many service workers aren’t&nbsp;
paid fairly to begin with. I mean, it’s not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:20.040
you tip service workers who are paid well. For&nbsp;
example, when was the last time you tipped your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.780
pilot or your lawyer or your accountant&nbsp;
or your doctor. These people provide way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:31.020
more valuable services than a waiter or a hotel&nbsp;
housekeeper, yet you don’t tip them. If anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:36.840
you might send them a thank you note or buy them&nbsp;
chocolate. Why? Well, probably because you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:41.700
that they’re already paid well which supports the&nbsp;
idea that the only reason you tip some service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:47.400
workers is because you know they’re paid poorly,&nbsp;
not actually because you appreciate their service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:52.920
So, theoretically, if we paid service workers&nbsp;
better wages, we can eliminate tipping culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:56.580
in America or atleast that’s what the anti tippers&nbsp;
think. Pro tippers don’t agree. They feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:01.980
eliminating tipping will simply give businesses&nbsp;
more opportunity to gauge prices. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:09.540
a $21 meal may rise to $24. Also, this isn’t all&nbsp;
that favorable for takeout customers as they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:14.880
also have to pay the higher prices. But, with&nbsp;
that being said, it looks like the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:21.180
world has made this work, so maybe there’s&nbsp;
something to it, but only time will tell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:27.060
Are you team anti tip or team pro tip? Comment&nbsp;
that down below. Also, drop a like if you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:33.060
tipping has gone too far. And of course, consider&nbsp;
